---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/site-layout.astro";
import { site } from "../site";
---
<SiteLayout title="Spyglass — Foundry" description="Explore a TypeScript workspace with Spyglass.">
  <article class="wrap product-page">
    <header>
      <p class="section-label">Spyglass</p>
      <h1>Get the lay of your code.</h1>
      <p class="product-page-intro">Scan a TypeScript workspace and explore its packages, files, and dependencies in a local map.</p>
      <a class="button" href={`${site.docs}spyglass/`}>Explore Spyglass</a>
    </header>
    <section>
      <h2>Start with a workspace</h2>
      <div>
        <p>Point Spyglass at a directory. It analyzes the workspace, saves the result outside it, and opens a local viewer. Packages become territories, files become settlements, and dependencies connect them.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Follow a question</h2>
      <div>
        <p>Explore a package, follow its dependencies, and inspect the signals that suggest a closer look. Findings are starting points for judgment, not verdicts about your code.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Take the analysis with you</h2>
      <div>
        <p>Print reports, check workspace rules, or export a static viewer. A scan reads the workspace; applying a planned change requires an explicit <code>--write</code>.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${site.docs}spyglass/reference/cli/`}>See the CLI reference →</a>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Run it locally</h2>
      <div>
        <p>Spyglass runs on Bun and can use Git history when Git is available. The documentation covers installation, scans, and the viewer.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${site.docs}spyglass/`}>Read the documentation →</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>
